yunohost (12.1.41.47) stable; urgency=low

  * nostrhost: reconcile native app web routes after every Caddy restart.
    - native app routes are pushed only through Caddy's admin API and never
      written to disk; any Caddy restart for any reason (a package upgrade
      that ships caddy.service and gets restarted by debhelper on unpack,
      a manual `systemctl restart caddy`, a crash-restart, a reboot) drops
      every installed native app back to the bare per-domain stub until
      something replays its route (found live: upgrading to 12.1.41.46 on
      the clean7 VM silently broke ditto, opencode-web_nh, nostrhost-test
      and configdemo all at once).
    - new `nostrhost app reconcile-routes`: best-effort, per-app, replays
      every installed native app's full stored manifest through
      package.reconcile. The resource engine already no-ops any operation
      whose current state still matches, so this only actually re-applies
      what Caddy lost (route, permission, health check); one app's failure
      never stops the rest from reconciling.
    - new nostrhost-web-reconcile.service (oneshot, After=/Requisite=
      caddy.service, no [Install] section - only ever triggered by
      caddy.service's own new Wants=nostrhost-web-reconcile.service, never
      independently enabled) runs it automatically every time Caddy starts.

 -- NostrHost <noreply@nostrhost.org>  Mon, 14 Sep 2026 09:00:00 +0000

yunohost (12.1.41.46) stable; urgency=low

  * nostrhost: allow --domain/--path overrides on native app install/upgrade.
    - native package.toml has no install-time question mechanism like
      classic _ynh's [install.domain]/[install.path]; every real _nh
      package (opencode-web_nh, nostrhost-test, ditto_nh, armada_nh)
      hardcoded a literal [web].domain, so installing on any other domain
      required hand-editing the checked-out package.toml first (found
      while test-installing ditto_nh on the clean7 VM).
    - `app install --domain/--path` substitute [web].domain/path (and keep
      health.path in sync when it tracked the old web.path) after
      manifest_sha256 verification, so an override can never forge what
      the catalogue actually signed.
    - `app upgrade --domain/--path` default to the currently installed
      values rather than package.toml's freshly-reloaded ones, so a
      routine upgrade never silently moves a live app back to a
      placeholder domain; deliberate moves still use `app change-url`.

